AB  - Recent advancements in deep learning have profoundly impacted various domains, including software development. This research primarily focuses on converting natural language descriptions into executable code - a transformative technique that converts natural language descriptions into executable code. Leveraging the T5 along with its specialized variant, CodeT5, this work aims to enhance developer productivity and code quality. To fine-tune these models, we utilize the MBPP (Mostly Basic Python Programming) benchmark dataset, ensuring their proficiency across diverse programming tasks. Traditional software development demands significant manual effort and technical expertise. Automating code generation from natural language inputs streamlines workflows, reduces errors, and democratizes coding. T5, originally designed for text-to-text tasks, and CodeT5, tailored for code intelligence, form the foundation of the robust text-to-code generation system. Through extensive mixed precision training, XLA compiler optimization, and distributed techniques, the model performance could be maximized. The MBPP dataset encompasses a wide range of Python programming problems, enabling our models to learn and generalize across various coding scenarios. We've developed utility functions and custom training loops to facilitate efficient data loading, model configuration, and training management. Assessments across various prediction tasks reveal the models' effectiveness in generating precise and functional code snippets from natural language inputs. Practical applications of this research include integration into Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) for real-time code suggestions, educational tools for programming instruction, and assistance in maintaining and refactoring legacy codebases. Our work underscores the transformative potential of natural language-to-code generation by demonstrating the performance of this model where it could be understood code quality is outstanding, as evidenced by the performance metrics. A BLEU score of 0.4 indicates the model's strong ability to produce high-quality code  © 2025 IEEE.
